{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>באש עלוהי</b> — it was evil in his eyes. <b>בל</b>: his heart, as in the Ishmaelite tongue <i>bel</i>. <b>לב להצילו</b> — to deliver him. <b>ועד בא השמש</b>: משתדר. It has no parallel [in Scripture], and its meaning is: like one plotting with all his ability and seeking pretexts."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase באש עלוהי is from Daniel 6:15 (‘it displeased him’); Ibn Ezra first glosses it, then moves to <b>בל</b>, likely the king's name Bel(shazzar) or the Aramaic for ‘heart/mind.’ <b>ועד בא השמש</b> (Daniel 6:15) is followed by the opaque one-word note משתדר, which may be a transliteration of an Aramaic form or a corrupt reading; rendered here as found."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:20:12.779548444Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}